Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:  

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be giants or dwarfs, so Vmag alone is not strongly indicative because it depends on distance.  
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means the star is closer; distance helps calculate absolute magnitude and luminosity class.  
- e_Plx (error in parallax): High error reduces reliability of distance and absolute magnitude; less useful directly for classification.  
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature and spectral class; giants tend to have certain B-V ranges depending on spectral type.  
- SpType (spectral type): Most direct indicator; giants have spectral types with III or III/IV suffixes, while dwarfs have V suffixes.  
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Very important; giants are more luminous and have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes than dwarfs of similar spectral type.  

Step 2. Based on the above and examples, inferred conditions:

10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant):  
- SpType is in [all types with suffix V only, e.g., G8V, F5V, A0V, K5V]  
- SpType is in [types with suffix IV/V or similar indicating subgiant/dwarf, e.g., G6/G8IV/V]  
- Amag (absolute magnitude) is >= 17  
- Amag is within range of [17, 21]  
- Plx is >= 5  
- B-V is within range of [0, 0.8]  
- Vmag is >= 7  
- e_Plx is <= 1.5 (reliable parallax, but star still classified as dwarf)  
- B-V is <= 0.7  
- Plx is within range of [5, 20]  

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant):  
- SpType is in [all types with suffix III or III/IV or III+ or similar, e.g., K2III, K0III, K1/K2III+, G8III, B3III, F3III, A3III]  
- Amag is < 17  
- Amag is within range of [11, 16.7]  
- Plx is <= 9.5  
- B-V is >= 1.0  
- B-V is within range of [1.0, 1.5]  
- Vmag is <= 8.9  
- e_Plx is <= 1.5 (parallax error acceptable)  
- Plx is within range of [0.7, 9.5]  
- B-V is >= 0.1 (giants tend to have redder colors, except early type giants)  

Summary:  
- The spectral type suffix is the strongest indicator: V means dwarf (class 0), III or III/IV means giant (class 1).  
- Absolute magnitude less than about 17 (brighter) indicates giant, greater or equal to 17 indicates dwarf.  
- B-V color index supports this: giants tend to have larger B-V (redder).  
- Parallax and apparent magnitude help indirectly by indicating distance and brightness.

