- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars have lower Vmag values. Giants can be bright but apparent magnitude depends on distance, so alone it is not a strong indicator.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star. Giants tend to be luminous and often farther away with smaller parallax, but this alone is not definitive.
- e_Plx (parallax error): High error reduces reliability of distance estimate, so less useful directly.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature/color. Giants can have a range of B-V but are often cooler (higher B-V) than main sequence stars of same spectral type.
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in spectral type (e.g., III, IV, V) directly indicates if a star is a giant (III), subgiant (IV), or main sequence (V). This is the most direct and reliable feature.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have brighter absolute magnitudes (lower numerical values) than dwarfs of the same spectral type. However, the examples here show very high Amag values, possibly due to calculation or data specifics, so relative comparison is key.

